The '''Acereria Nacional de Balboa''' (ANB; {{lang|ca|Acereria Nacional de Balboa}}; "National Steel Company of Balboa") was a [[state-owned enterprise|state-owned]] {{w|steel}} company established in 1949 to rebuild and operate the Balboan steel industry after the devastation of the [[Great Adonian War]]. The company was formed with [[Adonian Community]] reconstruction loans and matching funds from the Balboan rump state government, and it consolidated the remaining steel assets of the former [[Foneria General de Balboa]] (FGB).

The ANB operated two steelworks: the rebuilt Les Drassanes works at [[Les Drassanes]], near [[Portnou]], and a newer EAF plant at [[Sant Bart]] in the interior highlands. The company was the sole steel producer in Balboa throughout the AC mandate period and the post-mandate era. In 1982, as part of the broader privatisation programme, the ANB was split into two independent companies: [[Acereries de l'Istme]] (ACI), which inherited the Les Drassanes works, and [[Ferro Balboa]] (FBL), which inherited the Sant Bart plant.

== History ==

=== Establishment (1949) ===

The [[Great Adonian War]] (1935–1947) devastated the Balboan steel industry. The [[Foneria General de Balboa]] (FGB), the country's only steel producer, had been destroyed during the fighting — its blast furnaces sabotaged by retreating Zong forces in 1947 and its open hearth shop destroyed by aerial bombardment. By the end of the war, the FGB's production had fallen to zero, its Volisanian management had fled, and the works sat idle.

In 1949, the [[Adonian Community]] — which was preparing to assume administration of the former [[Zong Balboa]] territory — provided reconstruction loans to restart the Balboan steel industry. The Balboan rump state government contributed matching funds, and the two parties established the ANB as a state-owned conglomerate to consolidate the remaining steel assets. The ANB absorbed the ruins of the FGB, and the FGB was formally dissolved.

The ANB was established as a joint undertaking of the AC and the rump state government, reflecting the political complexity of the post-war period. The [[Adonian Community]] was preparing to assume administration of the former Zong Balboa territory, which would be divided into two mandate zones: the [[Adonian Community Mandate of the Balboa Canal Zone|Balboa Canal Zone mandate]], comprising the canal corridor and the western terminus at [[Portnou]], and the [[International Mandate of Sant Cristòfor|Sant Cristòfor mandate]], comprising the city of [[Sant Cristòfor]] and the territory north of the canal. The Balboan rump state, reduced to the interior highlands and the southern coast by the [[Treaty of Sant Llàtzer]] (1899), retained sovereignty over the territory surrounding the canal zone, including the interior highlands where [[Sant Bart]] served as the rump state capital.

=== Reconstruction of the Les Drassanes works (1949–1953) ===

The ANB's first priority was the reconstruction of the Les Drassanes works, which had been the core of the FGB's operations. The company began rebuilding the works with modern equipment imported from Volisania and Breisland, replacing the destroyed open hearth shop with a new {{w|basic oxygen steelmaking|basic oxygen furnace}} (BOF) shop and constructing a new {{w|blast furnace}} to replace the sabotaged units.

The rebuilt Les Drassanes works began production in 1953, during the AC mandate period (1950–1960), with a single blast furnace and the new BOF shop. The facility was the first modern steel plant in Balboa and represented a significant upgrade over the FGB's pre-war operations.

=== Establishment of the Sant Bart plant (1953) ===

Concurrently with the reconstruction of the Les Drassanes works, the ANB established a second steelworks at [[Sant Bart]], the rump state capital in the interior highlands. The Sant Bart plant was equipped with a small {{w|electric arc furnace}} (EAF) powered by hydropower from the nearby [[Yalabirga]] river system.

The Sant Bart plant was modest — producing approximately 100,000 tonnes per year — but it demonstrated the viability of EAF steelmaking in Balboa, where cheap hydropower could offset the cost of imported {{w|scrap metal}}. The plant's establishment in the rump state interior, rather than in the canal zone, reflected the political division of the post-war period: the Les Drassanes works was located in the AC mandate territory, while the Sant Bart plant was located in the rump state.

=== The mandate period (1950–1960) ===

During the AC mandate period, the ANB was the sole steel producer in Balboa. The mandate economy was separate from the rump state economy, and the ANB's two plants served different markets: the Les Drassanes works served the mandate territory, while the Sant Bart plant served the interior rump state.

The division of the ANB's operations across two administrative jurisdictions created logistical and managerial challenges. The Les Drassanes works, located in the [[Adonian Community Mandate of the Balboa Canal Zone|Balboa Canal Zone mandate]], was subject to AC administration, while the Sant Bart plant was subject to the rump state government. The ANB's management coordinated production and investment across both jurisdictions, but the political separation of the two territories limited the company's ability to optimise its operations.

After the mandate ended in 1960 and the Sant Cristòfor territory was returned to Balboa under the [[Treaty of Balboa Harbour]], the ANB's Les Drassanes works remained in the canal zone mandate, which was not returned to Balboa until 1973. The Sant Bart plant, meanwhile, continued to serve the interior, which was now part of the unified Balboan state.

=== Post-reunification (1973–1982) ===

The [[Treaty of Areza]] (1973) returned the canal zone to Balboan sovereignty, reunifying the country for the first time since 1899. The reunification brought the ANB's two plants under a single national administration for the first time in the company's history. The company invested in modernisation, expanding the Les Drassanes works with a second blast furnace and upgrading the Sant Bart EAF capacity.

By 1980, the ANB produced approximately 3.5 million tonnes of {{w|crude steel}} per year, with the Les Drassanes works accounting for approximately 3.0 million tonnes and the Sant Bart plant accounting for approximately 500,000 tonnes. The company employed approximately 6,000 people across both sites.

=== Privatisation (1982) ===

In 1982, as part of the broader privatisation programme that also saw the privatisation of [[Polímers de Balboa]] (1994) and [[Nutrició del Món]] (1988), the Balboan government privatised the ANB. The company was restructured and split into two independent companies:

* '''[[Acereries de l'Istme]]''' (ACI), which inherited the Les Drassanes works — the successor to the original FGB — and was listed on the [[Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor]] in 1983. The [[Camps family]], descendants of the original FGB founder Esteve Camps i Ribera, acquired a 10 per cent stake in the privatisation.
* '''[[Ferro Balboa]]''' (FBL), which inherited the Sant Bart EAF plant and was listed on the Bolsa in 1984. FBL was established as a pure EAF producer, taking advantage of Balboa's cheap hydropower and growing domestic scrap metal supply.

The privatisation opened the door to new entrants in the Balboan steel industry. In 1984, Pere Masdeu i Font, a metallurgical engineer who had worked at the ANB's Sant Bart plant, founded [[Grup Metal·lúrgic de Sant Bart]] (GMS) as a specialty steel producer.

== Operations ==

The ANB operated two steelworks with distinct technologies and markets:

=== Les Drassanes works ===

The rebuilt Les Drassanes works was the ANB's primary production facility. Located in the heavy industry municipality of [[Les Drassanes]], northwest of [[Portnou]], the works was an {{w|integrated steel plant|integrated steel mill}} using the blast furnace — basic oxygen furnace route. The plant was equipped with:

* '''1 × {{w|blast furnace}}''' (from 1953; a second was added after 1973)
* '''1 × {{w|basic oxygen steelmaking|basic oxygen furnace}} (BOF) shop'''
* '''{{w|rolling (metalworking)|Rolling mill}} complex''': Producing both long and flat products

The Les Drassanes works used imported {{w|iron ore}} and {{w|coking coal}} as raw materials. During the ANB period, iron ore was sourced primarily from [[Louyang]], reflecting the historical supply relationship established during the Zong Balboa period. The works served the mandate territory during the AC mandate period and the domestic market after reunification.

=== Sant Bart plant ===

The Sant Bart plant was the ANB's secondary facility, located in the interior highlands at [[Sant Bart]], the rump state capital. The plant was equipped with:

* '''1 × {{w|electric arc furnace}} (EAF)'''
* '''{{w|rolling (metalworking)|Bar rolling mill}}''': Producing long products

The Sant Bart plant used {{w|scrap metal}} as its raw material and was powered by hydropower from the [[Yalabirga]] river system. The plant served the rump state interior during the mandate period and the domestic market after reunification. The Sant Bart plant's EAF technology, which did not require imported iron ore or coking coal, was better suited to the rump state's limited import capacity and demonstrated the viability of EAF steelmaking in Balboa.

== Legacy ==

The ANB's legacy is as the institutional bridge between the colonial-era FGB and the modern Balboan steel industry. The company rebuilt the Les Drassanes works after the devastation of the Great Adonian War, established the Sant Bart EAF plant that demonstrated the viability of electric arc steelmaking in Balboa, and managed the steel industry through the politically complex mandate and post-mandate period.

The ANB's two successor companies — [[Acereries de l'Istme]] (ACI) and [[Ferro Balboa]] (FBL) — continue to operate the Les Drassanes and Sant Bart works respectively, and together with [[Grup Metal·lúrgic de Sant Bart]] (GMS), they produce approximately 14.29 million tonnes of crude steel per year.

